# wall — real-world examples

Broadcast a message to every logged-in terminal. Handy warning shot
before reboots on a shared box.

## Basics

```sh
# Pipe a message to everyone
echo "Good Morning Dave" | wall

# Broadcast the contents of a file
wall /etc/motd.maintenance

# Send without the "Broadcast message from ..." banner
echo "quick note" | wall -n
```

## Homelab uses

```sh
# Warn tmux/ssh sessions before a zypper dup + reboot on homelab
echo "homelab rebooting in 5 minutes for updates — save your work" | wall

# Same warning remotely on mediaserver
ssh david@mediaserver 'echo "Plex going down for maintenance at 22:00" | wall'

# Pair with shutdown, which broadcasts its own wall message automatically
sudo shutdown -r +5 "Kernel update — back in a couple minutes"

# Announce to a specific group only
echo "backup window starting" | wall -g users
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-n` | suppress the banner (root only) |
| `-g GROUP` | restrict broadcast to members of GROUP |
| `-t SEC` | write timeout per terminal |

Note: users can opt out of broadcasts with `mesg n`.
